diagnosis_text:
I could not reproduce a one‑way return‑path loss between the two sites. End‑to‑end ICMP from both directions is clean: pc1 -> pc2 and pc2 -> pc1 show 0% loss with sub‑millisecond RTTs. Stateful TCP also works bidirectionally: iperf from pc1→pc2 sustained ~136 Mbit/s and from pc2→pc1 ~134 Mbit/s, with normal retransmits and no stalls, confirming that replies are reaching the initiator. Routing is correct on both routers: FRR on router1 advertises 195.11.14.0/24 and learns 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2; router2 advertises 200.1.1.0/24 and learns 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1. Both routes are installed in the RIB and forwarded over eth0 on each side. Host defaults are correct (pc1 via 195.11.14.1, pc2 via 200.1.1.1). There is no filtering in iptables/nft on hosts or routers that would block return traffic; rp_filter is disabled and IP forwarding is enabled on both routers.

Affected component: none observed; path, routing, and forwarding are healthy.
Mechanism: Not reproducible—ICMP and TCP statefully traverse both ways, so no asymmetric drop is occurring now.
Localization: Entire path across router1 eth0 <-> router2 eth0 and access links is functioning as expected.
Root cause: No network-side fault currently present; prior symptom was likely transient or external to this network segment.
Corrective fix: No changes required. Recommend the user retest; if intermittency recurs, enable BFD on the eBGP session and add continuous bidirectional probes/alerts to catch transient return‑path issues. 
=== END ===